<div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Significance_and_applications">Significance and applications</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Steric_effects&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7" title="Edit section: Significance and applications"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>Steric effects are critical to <a href="/wiki/Chemistry" title="Chemistry">chemistry</a>, <a href="/wiki/Biochemistry" title="Biochemistry">biochemistry</a>, and <a href="/wiki/Pharmacology" title="Pharmacology">pharmacology</a>.  In organic chemistry, steric effects are nearly universal and affect the rates and activation energies of most <a href="/wiki/Chemical_reaction" title="Chemical reaction">chemical reactions</a> to varying degrees.
</p><p>In biochemistry, steric effects are often exploited in naturally occurring molecules such as <a href="/wiki/Enzyme" title="Enzyme">enzymes</a>, where the <a href="/wiki/Catalysis" title="Catalysis">catalytic</a> site may be buried within a large <a href="/wiki/Protein" title="Protein">protein</a> structure. In pharmacology, steric effects determine how and at what rate a <a href="/wiki/Medication" title="Medication">drug</a> will interact with its target bio-molecules.
</p>
